"2026-12-09","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Select Committee","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.runnymede.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-16","committee":"Full Council","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.runnymede.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-16","committee":"West Surrey Shadow Authority Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed; Extraordinary West Surrey Shadow Authority Overview & Scrutiny Committee","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.runnymede.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-01","committee":"Full Council","time":"7.00 pm","status":"New; Extraordinary","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.runnymede.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-10","committee":"West Surrey Shadow Authority Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.runnymede.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-22","committee":"Standards and Audit Committee","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.runnymede.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Runnymede's reserves forecast to fall to \u00a32m by 2032 as costs mount","lead":"Runnymede Council's General Fund working balance\u2014its main cushion against unexpected costs\u2014is forecast to fall from \u00a321.6m in March 2027 to just \u00a32.0m by March 2032, with reserves dropping \u00a311.7m between 2029 and 2031. The deterioration is driven by a \u00a31.2m recurring cost from the council's 2025 pay and grading review and a \u00a39.1m deficit this year on its housing revenue account, the separate account for council-owned homes. The council's \u00a325.9m annual rental income from its investment property portfolio is its largest revenue source, but it has acknowledged it must be wary about reletting properties at current rates. To address the pressures, the council is exploring early repayment of one loan, has agreed to sell properties to reduce borrowing and interest costs, and commissioned a 30-year housing asset management plan.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Q1 - Budget Monitoring report - Appenxix 6 v3 - final","paper_url":"https://runnymede.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s18927/18927.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Runnymede's reserves forecast to collapse to \u00a32m within six years as costs outpace income.","lead":"Runnymede council's general fund reserves, the financial cushion it holds for unexpected costs, are forecast to fall from \u00a321.6m next March to just \u00a32m by 2032, with the rate of depletion accelerating sharply from 2029 onwards. The council's housing account, funded by rental income from its own homes, is also in deficit, forecast to lose \u00a39.1m in a single year. The deterioration is driven by ongoing cost pressures, principally a pay and grading review that added \u00a31.241m to the annual budget. The council is exploring early repayment of loans using maturing investments and has agreed to dispose of several properties to reduce borrowing, but officers note that further savings and efficiencies would be needed if current forecasts materialise.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Q1 - Budget Monitoring report - Appenxix 6 v3 - final","paper_url":"https://runnymede.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s18927/18927.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Runnymede forecasts unsustainable reserve depletion: \u00a39m housing deficit and general fund to \u00a32m by 2032","lead":"Runnymede Council is forecasting unsustainable reserve depletion on two fronts. The housing revenue account, which funds the council's own homes from rental income, will run a deficit of \u00a39.1 million in 2026/27, reducing its working balance from \u00a325.3 million to \u00a315 million. The council's general reserves \u2014 money set aside for emergencies \u2014 are forecast to fall from \u00a321.6 million in March 2027 to \u00a32 million by March 2032, a pace the council describes as unsustainable. Cost pressures drive the decline, principally a \u00a31.241 million ongoing commitment from a pay and grading review. The council depends on \u00a325.9 million in annual investment property rental income, but officers acknowledge risk in reletting properties at current rental levels. The council is exploring property disposals to reduce borrowing, though timelines remain uncertain.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Q1 - Budget Monitoring report - Appenxix 6 v3 - final","paper_url":"https://runnymede.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s18927/18927.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Corporate Management Committee","paper_title":"CMC - Q1 Investment Property Update - Final","paper_url":"https://runnymede.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s18922/18922.pdf","headline":"Debt service coverage ratios improved to 2.55 (interest cover) and 1.87 (total debt cover), both in safe territory.","detail":"Interest cover ratio improved to 2.55 from 2.46 prior year; debt coverage ratio (including MRP principal repayment) improved to 1.87 from 1.83 prior year. Document states that ratios above 1.5 are considered safe; a ratio below 1 indicates borrower struggles to meet obligations. Both metrics now comfortably exceed safety threshold, indicating net rental income continues to cover combined interest and principal obligations despite asset value declines.","category":"borrowing","matters":"While LTV is acutely problematic, improving coverage ratios show cash flow from operations is sufficient to service debt obligations. This demonstrates the portfolio's income remains resilient despite market stress, supporting repayment capacity despite negative equity.","figures":"Interest cover 2.55 (from 2.46); debt cover 1.87 (from 1.83); safe threshold noted as 1.5","quote":"Debt cover ratio The total net income from property investments compared to the total annual MRP and interest on associated borrowings 1.87 (Previous Year 1.83)","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Corporate Management Committee","paper_title":"CMC - Q1 Investment Property Update - Final","paper_url":"https://runnymede.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s18922/18922.pdf","headline":"Property disposal programme achieved \u00a3556k surplus on six properties sold at 25.1% above valuation.","detail":"Six properties (four investment, two operational) completed sales in Q1 2026/27. Combined sale price \u00a32.775m against total valuations of \u00a32.218m, generating surplus of \u00a3556.7k (25.1% premium). Paper notes that \"in every case, sale prices have exceeded the reserve price set at auction, demonstrating strong market interest in the assets.\" Two further properties currently under offer. Disposals support council's objective of reducing borrowing.","category":"capital","matters":"Premium disposals provide capital receipts that directly reduce net debt and demonstrate effective asset management. Capital proceeds support deleveraging, partially offsetting LTV deterioration. However, auction outcomes may not represent sustainable ongoing pricing.","figures":"\u00a32.775m sale price vs \u00a32.218m valuation; \u00a3556.7k surplus; 25.1% premium","quote":"The six investment properties disposed of during the period achieved a combined sale price of \u00a32,775,000 against a total valuation of \u00a32,218,300, a surplus of \u00a3556,700, representing approximately 25.1% above the aggregate valuation.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Corporate Management Committee","paper_title":"CMC - Q1 Investment Property Update - Final","paper_url":"https://runnymede.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s18922/18922.pdf","headline":"Non-Statutory Best Value Notice issued in December 2023 for high debt has been withdrawn.","detail":"Council received Non-Statutory Best Value Notice from MHCLG in December 2023, with key comment citing concern over high debt. In response, council placed moratorium on new investment debt and pursued reduction strategy. The paper confirms the NS-BVN \"has now been withdrawn,\" indicating regulatory acceptance of remedial actions and removal of the notice condition.","category":"governance","matters":"A notice withdrawn is materially different from one outstanding. It confirms the council's remedial actions (borrowing halt, disposals strategy) have addressed regulator concerns, reducing systemic risk. This matters to lenders assessing regulatory standing and likelihood of further restrictions.","figures":"Notice issued December 2023; now withdrawn as of September 2026 meeting date","quote":"Following the non-statutory best value notice, which has now been withdrawn","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Corporate Management Committee","paper_title":"CMC - Q1 Investment Property Update - Final","paper_url":"https://runnymede.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s18922/18922.pdf","headline":"Annual capital value decline accelerated to \u00a310.9m, more than double the prior year, concentrated in office sector.","detail":"March 2026 revaluation shows capital values declining \u00a310.9m in the year, accelerating from \u00a33.8m decline in prior year. Major losses in office assets: AXIS One fell from \u00a319.3m to \u00a37.7m; 610 Chiswick High Road from \u00a365.1m to \u00a332.9m; Scandinavia House from \u00a312m to \u00a34.66m; New Globe House from \u00a316.7m to \u00a38.25m; Pine Trees Estate from \u00a380.7m to \u00a336.56m.","category":"capital","matters":"Accelerating rather than stabilizing losses indicate ongoing market exposure problems. Concentrated losses in office sector suggest structural weakness in demand rather than cyclical downturn, with material risk of further declines.","figures":"\u00a310.9m annual decline (from \u00a33.8m prior year); \u00a3177.5m total losses since purchase","quote":"Difference in Capital Values, annually. \u00a3-10,910,000 (Previous year - \u00a3-3,847,500)","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Corporate Management Committee","paper_title":"CMC - Q1 Investment Property Update - Final","paper_url":"https://runnymede.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s18922/18922.pdf","headline":"Loan-to-value ratio deteriorated to 137%, meaning the council owes more on borrowing than the investment properties are worth.","detail":"The LTV ratio worsened from 133% to 137%, indicating the council now has negative equity in the investment property portfolio. A ratio above 100% means the value of assets has fallen below the amounts borrowed. The portfolio has accumulated total losses of \u00a3177.5m since purchase, representing the core structural problem cited in the December 2023 Best Value Notice.","category":"borrowing","matters":"LTV above 100% is a fundamental credit concern for lenders: the borrower has negative equity and lacks capacity to repay through asset sale. Every further valuation decline worsens the position for the creditor.","figures":"137% (from 133% prior year); \u00a3177.5m cumulative losses since purchase","quote":"Loan to Value ratio Amount of debt compared to the total asset value. 137% (Previous Year \u2013 133%)","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-24"},"name":"Runnymede","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-26","since_iso":"2026-08-27","distress":true,"headline":"Debt service coverage ratios improved to 2.55 (interest cover) and 1.87 (total debt cover), both in safe territory.","lead":"While LTV is acutely problematic, improving coverage ratios show cash flow from operations is sufficient to service debt obligations. This demonstrates the portfolio's income remains resilient despite market stress, supporting repayment capacity despite\u2026","also":["27 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Overview and Scrutiny Select Committee on 2026-10-06","n_findings":28,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"CMC - Q1 Investment Property Update - Final","top_paper_url":"https://runnymede.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s18922/18922.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":115,"news_drafts_total":3}}